# Normalizes line endings for all text files to LF, regardless of which OS
# or editor last touched them.
#
# This is what caused the "137 changed files" confusion: without a policy
# like this, git has no consistent rule, so whichever tool most recently
# touched a file (a sandbox, a local editor, Windows vs Linux) silently
# decides its line endings. Every line in that file then reads as "changed"
# to git even when the actual code is identical - which is exactly why most
# of those 137 files looked the same but still showed as modified.
#
# NOTE: the first time this file is committed to a repository that still has
# CRLF files in its history (as this project's did, being originally
# authored on Windows), git will want to renormalize those files to match -
# expect a one-time commit that touches a lot of files. After that, this
# stops happening for good.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly mark binary types so git never attempts text/EOL conversion on
# them under any configuration, no matter what * text=auto above guesses.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.svg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
